zPotential # Closed non-displaced radial head fracture from fall from standing to elbow
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Focal radial head tenderness, Pain exacerbated by supination/pronation, FOOSH mechanism with positive fat pad sign

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-047
Reason for Visit: Acute elbow pain and restricted movement following a fall, requiring urgent assessment for a displaced intra-articular fracture or dislocation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a fall onto an outstretched hand resulting in an inability to fully extend or rotate the elbow. Urgent clinical evaluation was necessary to rule out elbow dislocation, clear a displaced mechanical fracture, and apply supportive immobilization prior to obtaining definitive daytime radiography.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I fell on my hand and now I can't straighten my arm, and twisting my wrist sends a sharp pain through my elbow.
History of Present Illness: Patient provides a history of a fall from a standing height onto an extended arm with an outstretched hand (FOOSH) 3 hours ago. This resulted in focal, intense bony tenderness localized precisely over the radial head in the lateral elbow. The pain is distinctly reproduced and severely exacerbated during active or passive forearm supination and pronation. There is marked restriction of full active elbow extension, primarily due strictly to pain rather than mechanical blocking.
Functional Status: Fully independent in ambulation; holding the affected arm flexed across the chest.
Social/Safety Concerns: Minor trip hazard located in the home; advised regarding safe walking spaces.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 132/80 | HR: 80 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Alert, in moderate localized distress when moving the arm, guarding the elbow in 90 degrees of flexion.
Physical Exam: Elbow examination reveals focal radial head tenderness and a moderate localized effusion resulting in fullness specifically in the lateral soft tissue gutter. There is an absence of gross joint deformity, instability, or visible obvious osseous angulation. Pain is exacerbated by supination/pronation. Distal neurovascular examination of the hand is intact, including normal radial, ulnar, and median nerve profiles. There is pain-free palpation of the olecranon, medial epicondyle, and distal radius/ulna. Normal strong grip strength is maintained in the affected limb.
Environmental Observations: Loose floorboard identified as the trip hazard.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Potential closed non-displaced radial head fracture - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Elbow dislocation - Ruled out due to: Intact anatomical triangle of the elbow (olecranon and epicondyles) without gross structural deformity or extreme vascular compromise.

Distal radius fracture (Colles/Smith) - Ruled out due to: Complete absence of wrist pain, deformity, and negative point tenderness over the distal radius.

Olecranon fracture - Ruled out due to: Absence of focal bony tenderness over the posterior elbow and preserved, albeit painful, triceps extension mechanism.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

A FOOSH injury causing painful restriction of forearm rotation is highly suspicious for a radial head fracture. Bedside clinical assessment confidently excluded a major joint dislocation, olecranon fracture, and neurovascular compromise, isolating the pathology to the proximal radioulnar joint. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically required to rule out these surgical emergencies, provide immediate and effective sling immobilization to halt the severe pain cycle, and bridge the patient safely to standard daytime radiography, successfully bypassing an unnecessary overnight emergency department stay.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Fitted the patient with a broad arm sling to support the elbow at 90 degrees of flexion. Prescribed scheduled oral NSAIDs to reduce the acute intra-articular effusion. Explicitly instructed the patient to obtain anteroposterior and lateral elbow radiographs tomorrow to visualize the suspected fracture and look for an elevated anterior/posterior fat pad sign.
Positive Behavioral Change: Keep the arm resting securely in the sling at 90 degrees. This immediately unloads the tension on the elbow capsule, actively preventing the fractured bone ends from rubbing together during accidental movement, directly stopping the sharp, shooting pain.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Broad arm sling.
Follow-up: Routine GP review tomorrow with the requested elbow x-ray to definitively confirm the diagnosis and arrange physiotherapy or orthopedic review.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed immediately to the Emergency Department if your hand suddenly becomes cold, pale, or completely numb, or if you develop an agonizing, tight, and unremitting pain in the forearm muscle that does not respond to painkillers.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Radial head fractures are often extremely subtle and may only show as a "fat pad sign" on an x-ray, representing bleeding inside the joint.
Clinical Course: Non-displaced radial head fractures are typically treated in a sling for just a few days before early movement is encouraged to prevent the elbow from becoming permanently stiff.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention if your fingers turn blue or white, or if the pain suddenly becomes severely worse and spreads down to your wrist.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A fall on an outstretched hand rams the forearm bone directly into the elbow joint, often causing a tiny hairline crack in the radial head. The sling rests the joint to let the swelling settle.
Actionable Steps: Get the requested elbow x-ray tomorrow. Wear the sling continuously for now. Sleep slightly propped up and rest your arm on a pillow.

